# Rust — CursorRules Pro

You are an expert Rust developer. Follow these rules strictly.

## Project Conventions
- Use Rust 2021 edition. Target the latest stable toolchain.
- Project layout: `src/` with `main.rs` or `lib.rs`, organized into modules with `mod.rs` or file-per-module.
- Use a workspace (`Cargo.toml` with `[workspace]`) for multi-crate projects.
- Run `cargo clippy` and `cargo fmt` before every commit. Fix all warnings.
- Document all public APIs with `///` doc comments. Include examples in doc comments.

## Ownership & Borrowing
- Prefer borrowing (`&T`, `&mut T`) over ownership transfer unless the function needs to own the data.
- Use `Clone` sparingly — it signals a design problem if cloning is frequent.
- Prefer `&str` over `String` in function parameters. Accept `impl AsRef<str>` for maximum flexibility.
- Return owned types (`String`, `Vec<T>`) from functions that create new data.
- Use lifetimes explicitly when the compiler cannot infer them. Prefer named lifetimes over `'_`.

## Error Handling
- Define domain-specific error types with `thiserror`: `#[derive(Debug, thiserror::Error)]`.
- Use `anyhow::Result` in application code, custom error types in library code.
- Never use `.unwrap()` or `.expect()` in production code — use `?` operator or match.
- Use `.unwrap()` only in tests and examples. Comment why if the unwrap is intentional.
- Map errors at boundaries: convert library errors to application errors with context.

## Type System & Patterns
- Use the newtype pattern to add type safety: `struct UserId(Uuid)` instead of raw `Uuid`.
- Prefer `enum` for state machines and variant types. Use exhaustive matching.
- Implement `Display` and `Debug` for all public types.
- Use `From`/`Into` traits for type conversions. Implement `TryFrom` for fallible conversions.
- Use `Builder` pattern for complex struct construction. Derive `Default` when sensible.

## Traits & Generics
- Define traits for behavior abstraction. Keep traits small and focused (ISP).
- Use `impl Trait` in argument position for simple cases, generics for complex bounds.
- Prefer static dispatch (`impl Trait`) over dynamic dispatch (`dyn Trait`) unless you need heterogeneous collections.
- Use trait objects (`Box<dyn Trait>`) for plugin architectures and runtime polymorphism.
- Implement standard traits: `Debug`, `Clone`, `PartialEq`, `Eq`, `Hash` where applicable.

## Async Rust
- Use `tokio` as the async runtime. Annotate main with `#[tokio::main]`.
- Use `async fn` and `.await` — never block the async runtime with synchronous I/O.
- Use `tokio::spawn` for concurrent tasks. Use `tokio::select!` for racing futures.
- Wrap blocking code with `tokio::task::spawn_blocking`.
- Use channels (`tokio::sync::mpsc`) for inter-task communication.

## Memory & Performance
- Prefer stack allocation over heap allocation. Use `Box<T>` only when needed.
- Use `Cow<'_, str>` for functions that sometimes need to own and sometimes borrow.
- Use `Vec::with_capacity()` when the size is known upfront.
- Prefer iterators over index-based loops — they optimize better and are more idiomatic.
- Use `#[inline]` sparingly — let the compiler decide. Profile before optimizing.

## Concurrency
- Use `Arc<Mutex<T>>` for shared mutable state across threads. Prefer `RwLock` for read-heavy loads.
- Use `crossbeam` for advanced concurrent data structures.
- Avoid `Rc` in multi-threaded code — use `Arc` instead.
- Use `Send` and `Sync` bounds explicitly when defining async interfaces.

## Testing
- Write unit tests in the same file with `#[cfg(test)] mod tests { ... }`.
- Write integration tests in `tests/` directory for public API testing.
- Use `#[should_panic]` for expected failure tests. Use `assert_matches!` for pattern testing.
- Use `proptest` or `quickcheck` for property-based testing of complex logic.
- Mock traits with `mockall` crate for dependency injection in tests.

## Common Mistakes to Avoid
- Do NOT use `.unwrap()` in production code — handle errors explicitly.
- Do NOT fight the borrow checker — redesign if you are using `Rc<RefCell<T>>` everywhere.
- Do NOT use `unsafe` unless absolutely necessary — document why and minimize scope.
- Do NOT clone to satisfy the borrow checker — restructure ownership instead.
- Do NOT ignore compiler warnings — they often indicate real bugs.
- Do NOT use `String` where `&str` suffices — excessive allocation hurts performance.
